Please make a targeted correction to the newly added Section 6
“Liability-Side Treatment: Dealer-Posted G10 Bond Collateral.”

IMPORTANT: this instruction supersedes the liability-side methodology in my
previous prompt.

The previous instruction incorrectly made

    q_repo^G10 B_post (r_CoF - r_repo^G10)

the starting/base liability-side VA.

That expression may describe an opportunity-cost component associated with
encumbering an otherwise repoable G10 bond, but it is NOT the correct starting
point for the liability-side FVA methodology because it loses the base
valuation benchmark r_base.

The liability side should instead be anchored in the standard/classic FVA
treatment of a dealer funding a collateral-posting obligation.

Please revise Section 6 accordingly.

Do NOT modify the established asset-side methodology.

================================================================
1. START FROM THE CLASSIC CASH-COLLATERAL FVA BENCHMARK
================================================================

Define dealer liability exposure:

    L_t = (-V_t)^+.

When L_t > 0, the dealer owes collateral to the counterparty.

The clean/base derivative valuation is collateral-independent and uses the
approved clean discounting convention:

    r_base = RFR

(subject to the desk/methodology confirmation already discussed).

The correct conceptual starting point is the standard cash-posting case.

If the dealer were required to post L_t of cash and funds that cash at its
marginal unsecured cost of funds r_CoF, then under the paper's sign convention
(negative VA = cost):

    VA_L^cash
      = ∫ E[
          DF_t
          L_t (r_base,t - r_CoF,t)
        ] dt.

This is the classic liability-side FCA/FVA benchmark.

Make this equation the anchor for the entire liability-side discussion.

The economic interpretation is:

    base valuation assumes funding at r_base
        versus
    actual dealer funding of the collateral posting at r_CoF.

If r_CoF > r_base, the adjustment is negative.

================================================================
2. THEN INTRODUCE G10 BOND POSTING AS THE NON-CASH EXTENSION
================================================================

The actual transaction permits/requires the dealer to post eligible G10 bonds
rather than cash.

Let

    q_CSA^G10

be the CSA valuation percentage for the G10 collateral.

Then:

    B_t^post
      = L_t / q_CSA^G10.

Make clear:

    q_CSA determines how much G10 bond market value must be posted.

It does NOT by itself determine the funding cost.

The methodological question is now:

    How does satisfying the L_t collateral obligation with G10 bonds change
    the dealer's funding economics relative to the classic cash-posting
    benchmark?

This should be treated as the non-cash extension of standard liability-side
FVA, NOT as a completely separate opportunity-cost framework.

================================================================
3. CORRECT THE ROLE OF r_repo AND THE OPPORTUNITY-COST ARGUMENT
================================================================

Retain the useful observation from the previous draft that posting an owned
G10 bond encumbers an asset that may otherwise have been repoable.

If an unencumbered G10 bond could otherwise be repoed with:

    q_repo^G10 = repo advance rate
    r_repo^G10 = G10 repo rate,

then the amount of repo funding capacity associated with the posted bond is:

    F_t^repo
      = q_repo^G10 B_t^post

      = (q_repo^G10 / q_CSA^G10) L_t.

Posting the bond removes that alternative secured-funding capacity.

However, DO NOT present

    F_t^repo (r_CoF - r_repo^G10)

as the complete liability-side VA.

It contains no r_base and therefore measures an incremental opportunity-cost
comparison between secured and unsecured funding rather than the complete
valuation adjustment relative to the base valuation.

The paper should distinguish explicitly between:

    A. the base-to-actual funding adjustment; and

    B. any incremental opportunity cost associated with choosing/encumbering
       G10 bonds rather than cash or another collateral form.

Do not combine these mechanically until the precise Treasury/balance-sheet
funding mechanics have been established.

================================================================
4. USE CASH POSTING AS A REQUIRED CONSISTENCY / LIMITING TEST
================================================================

Add an explicit methodological sanity check.

Any general liability-side non-cash methodology must reduce to the standard
cash result when collateral becomes cash or economically equivalent to cash:

    VA_L^cash
      = ∫ E[
          DF_t
          L_t (r_base - r_CoF)
        ] dt.

Therefore any proposed general formula that contains only

    r_CoF - r_repo

and no r_base cannot by itself represent the complete liability-side VA.

Use this as a consistency condition on any subsequent G10 formulation.

================================================================
5. DO NOT OVER-DERIVE THE G10 EFFECT IN THIS PAPER
================================================================

For the present white paper, we only need a BASE-CASE liability-side
discussion.

Do not manufacture a detailed production model for G10 sourcing if the
economics have not been confirmed.

The section should establish:

    (1) classic liability FVA starts from
            L_t (r_base - r_CoF);

    (2) G10 posting changes the actual funding economics because the dealer
        posts securities rather than cash;

    (3) for owned/readily sourced G10 bonds, the relevant incremental effect
        can be viewed through the opportunity cost of encumbering otherwise
        repoable securities;

    (4) q_repo and r_repo therefore help characterize that opportunity cost,
        but do not replace r_base as the valuation benchmark;

    (5) the exact mapping from G10 encumbrance into an effective posting
        funding rate should be confirmed with Treasury / methodology if the
        liability side becomes material.

If useful, introduce a generic effective posting funding rate:

    r_post^G10

and write the structurally correct general form as:

    VA_L^G10
      = ∫ E[
          DF_t
          L_t (r_base,t - r_post,t^G10)
        ] dt.

Then explain that determining r_post^G10 from repo opportunity cost,
inventory/sourcing economics, haircuts and Treasury treatment is a separate
implementation/calibration question.

Do NOT assign a final formula for r_post^G10 unless it follows rigorously from
the confirmed funding mechanics.

================================================================
6. CURRENT TRANSACTION: THIS IS LIKELY IMMATERIAL
================================================================

This remains an important part of the previous Section 6 and should be
preserved.

The liability-side adjustment is activated only by:

    L_t = (-V_t)^+.

Preliminary inspection of the swap exposure suggests dealer-negative exposure
may be negligible for the current transaction.

However, do not conclude this merely from positive expected exposure.

Confirm using the negative-exposure profile, e.g.

    ENE_t = E[(-V_t)^+],

or the pathwise exposure distribution.

If:

    ENE_t ≈ 0,

then:

    B_t^post ≈ 0

and therefore:

    VA_L ≈ 0

regardless of the finer distinction between cash funding and G10
opportunity-cost treatment.

This should be the practical conclusion for the current trade if confirmed.

================================================================
7. KEEP THE LIABILITY SECTION SHORT
================================================================

This correction should make Section 6 simpler, not longer.

A clean structure would be:

    6. Liability-Side Treatment

    6.1 Classic cash-posting benchmark
        L = (-V)^+
        VA_L^cash = ∫ DF L(r_base-r_CoF) dt

    6.2 G10 bond posting
        B_post = L/q_CSA^G10
        explain non-cash extension and encumbrance/opportunity cost

    6.3 Current transaction
        check ENE
        if ENE ≈ 0 -> liability VA ≈ 0

    6.4 Scope
        detailed G10 sourcing / repo specialness / Treasury transfer pricing
        outside scope unless liability exposure becomes material.

Aim for approximately one page, or at most 1–2 pages.

================================================================
8. TERMINOLOGY / CURVE DISCIPLINE
================================================================

Maintain the following distinctions:

    r_base / RFR
        clean valuation and VA benchmark;

    r_CoF
        dealer marginal unsecured funding rate;

    r_repo^G10
        secured funding rate available against the G10 bond;

    q_CSA^G10
        contractual valuation percentage determining bond quantity;

    q_repo^G10
        repo advance rate determining secured funding capacity;

    G10 bond yield / ASW
        asset-pricing characteristic of the G10 security, NOT automatically
        the swap discount rate or dealer funding rate.

Do not introduce q_reg.

Do not use the G10 bond ASW as the swap discount spread.

Do not reintroduce RFR+5bp unless its economic meaning has separately been
established.

Most importantly, preserve the conceptual hierarchy:

    BASE VALUATION
        RFR / r_base
            |
            v
    dealer liability exposure L=(-V)^+
            |
            v
    collateral posting obligation
            |
       -------------
       |           |
      cash       G10 bond
       |           |
    classic      non-cash posting /
    FCA          encumbrance economics
       \           /
        \         /
            v
    actual funding cost relative to r_base
            |
            v
        liability-side VA

Please explicitly correct any equations or prose introduced from the prior
prompt that imply that (r_CoF-r_repo) alone is the complete liability-side
valuation adjustment.
